+### View Matrix Construction
+Focuses on the transformation from camera space to world space using rotation and translation [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
+### Projection Matrix Construction
+Focuses on mapping 3D points to 2D screen coordinates using FOV and near/far planes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per camera setup, as it involves a fixed number of matrix multiplications and trigonometric operations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ additional memory for caching matrices and parameters [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Optimized for Metal GPU rendering, minimizing CPU-GPU synchronization by caching matrices [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Handles downscaling automatically, integrates SH degree calculation, and uses a custom projection matrix suitable for the specific training pipeline [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Tied to specific near/far plane values ($0.001, 1000.0$) which may not be suitable for all scenes [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ for matrix construction, assuming fixed-size matrices.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for storing matrices and cached values.
+- **Performance:** Efficient for real-time rendering and training due to caching and simple arithmetic [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Handles multi-resolution training via downscaling; explicit control over near/far planes [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Specific to the Metal/OpenGL context described; may not generalize to all projection types without modification [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting training, specifically in the Msplat implementation which aims for fast training on Apple Silicon using Metal [S1, unofficial]. It adapts standard camera setup procedures to the needs of Gaussian Splatting, including specific projection matrix construction and SH degree management [S1, unofficial].
+Originated in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting training and slice-based volume rendering [S1, S2, unofficial]. The method is implemented in projects like Msplat and Slice-Base-Volume-Rendering [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
